== Newton–Raphson Method

== The Orbit of $z_0$
#definition-box(outlined: false)[The Orbit of $z_0$][
  For the function $f_c$, defined by $f_c (z)=z^2+c$, and an initial seed $z_0$, the sequence of iterates given by $ z_1=f_c (z_0), z_2=f_c (z_1), ... $ is called the _orbit_ of $z_0$ generated by $f_c$.
] <def:orbit>
#set footnote(numbering: n => [])
#footnote[@hmca]

== Julia Set
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Julia set][
  We let $K_c$ denote the set of points with a bounded orbit for $f_c$. The boundary of $K_c$, denoted by $J_c$, is known as the _Julia set_ of the function $f_c$.
] <def:julia>
#footnote[@hmca]
#speaker-note[
  + If the orbit is not bounded, we say it _escapes to infinity_.
  + $K_c$ is referred to as the _filled Julia set_ for $f_c$.
  + The _basin of infinity_ for $f_c$ is where the orbit escapes to infinity, i.e., the complement of filled Julia set.
]

== Julia Set Connectedness
#proposition-box(outlined: false)[
  The boundary of $K_c$ is connected if and only if $0 in K_c$. In other words, the Julia set of $f_c$ is connected if and only if the orbit of $0$ is bounded.
]
#footnote[@hmca, Theorem 4.9]
#pagebreak(weak: true)
#set-qed-symbol[Q.E.D.]
#proof[]

== Mandelbrot Set
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Mandelbrot set][
  We let $M$ denote the set of complex numbers $c$ for which the Julia set of $f_c$ is connected, or equivalently, for which the orbit of $0$ under $f_c$ is bounded. The set $M$ is known as the _Mandelbrot set_.
] <def:mandelbrot>
#footnote[@hmca]

== Points
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Fixed point][
  The point $z_0$ is a _fixed point_ for the function $f$ if $ f(z_0)=z_0. $
]
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Attracting point][
  The point $z_0$ is an _attracting point_ for the function $f$ if $ |f'(z_0)|<1. $
]
#footnote[@hmca, Definition 4.9 and Definition 4.10]

== Local Attraction near an Attracting Fixed Point
#proposition-box(outlined: false)[
  Suppose that $z_0$ is an attracting fixed point for the function $f$. Then there is a disk $D_r (z_0)$ about $z_0$ such that the iterates of all the points in $D_r^*(z_0)$ are drawn toward $z_0$ in the sense that, if $z in D_r^*(z_0)$, then $ |f(z)-z_0|<|z-z_0|. $
]
#footnote[@hmca, Theorem 4.10]
#speaker-note[
  + In fact, if $z_k$ is the $k$th iterate of $z in D_r^*(z_0)$, then $ limits(lim)_(k -> oo) z_k=z_0. $
]
#pagebreak(weak: true)
#proof[
  As $z_0$ is an attracting fixed point for $f$, we know $f$ is differentiable at $z_0$ and $ |f'(z_0)|<1. $ It follows for any $epsilon>0$, that there exists some $r>0$ such that if $z in D_r^*(z_0)$, then $ abs(frac(f(z)-f(z_0), z-z_0)-f'(z_0))<epsilon. $ Take $epsilon=1-|f'(z_0)|>0$, then for $z in D_r^*(z_0)$, we have $ abs(frac(f(z)-f(z_0), z-z_0)-f'(z_0))<1-|f'(z_0)|. $ Observe that, by the triangle inequality, we obtain $ abs(frac(f(z)-f(z_0), z-z_0))&<=abs(frac(f(z)-f(z_0), z-z_0)-f'(z_0))+|f'(z_0)|\ &<1-|f'(z_0)|+|f'(z_0)|\ &=1. $ Multiplying both sides by $|z-z_0|$, we obtain $ |f(z)-f(z_0)|<|z-z_0|. $ Consequently, as $z_0$ is a fixed point of $f$, it must be that $f(z_0)=z_0$, so $ |f(z)-z_0|<|z-z_0|. $
]

== Characterization of Attracting Fixed Points
#proposition-box(outlined: false)[
  The function $f_c (z)=z^2+c$ has attracting fixed points if and only if $ |1+sqrt(1-4c)|<1 quad "or" quad |1-sqrt(1-4c)|<1, $ where the square root designates the principal square root function.
]
#footnote[@hmca, Theorem 4.11]
#pagebreak(weak: true)
#proof[
  Consider that $z_0$ is a fixed point for $f_c$ if and only if $f_c (z_0)=z_0$, i.e., if and only if $ z_0^2 -z_0+c=0. $ Then, by the quadratic formula, we obtain $ z_0=frac(1+sqrt(1-4c),2) quad "or" quad z_0=frac(1-sqrt(1-4c),2). $ Likewise, consider that $z_0$ is an attracting point for $f_c$ if and only if $|f_c '(z_0)|<1$. Observe that $f_c '(z)=2z$, so $ |f_c '(z_0)|=2z_0<1. $ Substituting the two previous solutions, we obtain $ |1+sqrt(1-4c)|<1 quad "or" quad |1-sqrt(1-4c)|<1. $ Consequently, $f_c (z)=z^2+c$ has attracting fixed points if and only if $ |1+sqrt(1-4c)|<1 quad "or" quad |1-sqrt(1-4c)|<1. $
]

== Cycles
#definition-box(outlined: false)[$n$-cycle][
  An _$n$-cycle_ for a function $f$ is a sequence ${z_0, z_1, ..., z_(n-1)}$ of $n$ distinct complex numbers such that $ z_k=f(z_(k-1)), quad 1<=k<=n-1 $ and $ f(z_(n-1))=z_0. $
] <def:cycle>
#footnote[@hmca, Definition 4.11]

== Attracting Cycles
#definition-box(outlined: false)[Attracting $n$-cycles][
  An $n$-cycle ${z_0, z_1, ..., z_(n-1)}$ for a function $f$ is said to be _attracting_ if $ |g_n '(z_0)|<1, $ where $g_n$ is the composition of $f$ with itself $n$ times.
]
#footnote[@hmca, Definition 4.12]

